{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"potential-powellite-mouflon","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## Context / Problem\nEvery LLM-tier MCP tool failure is reported with the single error code `llm_unavailable`,\nincluding failures that have nothing to do with LLM availability. A driving agent that\nbranches on the error code — which is exactly what the structured failure exists for — cannot\ntell \"the model backend is down, retry later\" from \"you named a workflow that does not exist,\nfix your input\".\n\nObserved 2026-08-27 against the LIVE MCP server (`https://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/mcp`),\nwhere `REBAR_MCP_ALLOW_LLM=1` and the LLM tier is fully enabled:\n\n```\nrender_workflow    {\"workflow\": \"nonexistent-wf\"}\n  -> {\"error\": \"llm_unavailable\",\n      \"message\": \"nonexistent-wf: workflow 'nonexistent-wf' not found (no such file,\n                  no .rebar/workflows/nonexistent-wf.yaml, and no built-in example of that name)\"}\n\nget_workflow_status {\"run_id\": \"nonexistent-run\"}\n  -> {\"error\": \"llm_unavailable\",\n      \"message\": \"unknown run_id 'nonexistent-run': no run-index entry and no ticket_id given\"}\n```\n\nBoth are caller-input errors on tools documented as always-available reads that make no LLM\ncall at all (`render_workflow`: \"Read tool, always available\"; `get_workflow_status`: \"Typed\nread tool ... (no execution)\"). Neither is an availability problem, and neither is retryable in\nthe sense the field implies.\n\n## Mechanism\n`src/rebar/_mcp_llm.py:21-37`\n\n```python\ndef _structured_llm_failure(exc: Exception) -> dict:\n    ...\n    o = outcome_of(exc)\n    return {\n        \"error\": \"llm_unavailable\",\n        \"message\": str(exc),\n        \"resolution_class\": o.resolution_class.value if o is not None else None,\n        \"retryable\": bool(o.retryable) if o is not None else False,\n        \"diagnostic\": o.diagnostic if o is not None else None,\n    }\n```\n\nThe `error` code is a hard-coded literal applied to every `LLMError` the tier raises,\nregardless of cause. When the raised error carries no classifier outcome (`outcome_of`\nreturns `None`) — which is the case for a not-found lookup — the caller additionally gets\n`resolution_class: null`, `retryable: false`, `diagnostic: null`, so there is no other field\nto disambiguate from either. The docstring's stated purpose (\"The driving agent can then\nbranch on `retryable` ... instead of string-parsing an error\") is defeated: string-parsing\n`message` is the only way to recover the real cause.\n\n## Impact\nThe MCP failure contract (`docs/mcp-reference.md`, \"Failure shape\") promises a\nmachine-readable identity drawn from `rebar.KNOWN_ERROR_CODES` that lets a client tell a\npolicy refusal from a fault. For the LLM tier that promise does not hold: an unrecoverable\ncaller mistake and a transient backend outage are indistinguishable without natural-language\nparsing, so an agent will either retry a permanently-broken call or escalate a typo.\n\n## Reproduction\n1. Against an MCP server with `REBAR_MCP_ALLOW_LLM=1`, call `render_workflow` with a workflow\n   name that does not exist.\n2. Observe `error: \"llm_unavailable\"` with `resolution_class: null`, `retryable: false`.\n3. Repeat with `get_workflow_status` and an unknown `run_id`; observe the same code.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] AC1: A not-found workflow name from `render_workflow` returns an error code distinct from `llm_unavailable` that identifies it as a caller-input/not-found error.\n- [ ] AC2: An unknown `run_id` from `get_workflow_status` likewise returns a not-found error code, not `llm_unavailable`.\n- [ ] AC3: A genuine LLM backend unavailability (no API key, or a provider error) still returns `llm_unavailable` with the classifier fields populated.\n- [ ] AC4: Every error code emitted by `_structured_llm_failure` is a member of `rebar.KNOWN_ERROR_CODES`, matching the failure-shape contract in `docs/mcp-reference.md`.\n- [ ] AC5: Regression tests cover AC1, AC2, and AC3 — asserting on the `error` code, not the message text.\n\n## Definition of Done\n- [ ] DD1: AC1-AC4 implemented; AC5 tests pass.\n- [ ] DD2: `docs/mcp-reference.md` failure-shape section names the LLM-tier codes if the set changes.\n","id":"dbca-97ac-ad96-4d6d","parent_id":"","priority":3,"tags":["mcp","errors"],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"MCP LLM-tier tools report caller-input and not-found failures as llm_unavailable, defeating error-code branching"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787815542326671993,"uuid":"7dd813d1-33d4-4046-98d6-a4cc5800a671"}